    "id": "nicastro",
    "short": "Reading · Nicastro & Carillo",
    "title": "Food loss and waste prevention strategies",
    "cite": "Nicastro & Carillo (2021)",
    "kind": "Core reading",
    "reference": "Adapted from: Nicastro, R., & Carillo, P. (2021). Food loss and waste prevention strategies from farm to fork. Sustainability, 13(10), Article 5443. https://doi.org/10.3390/su13105443",
    "citeAs": "Cite as Nicastro and Carillo (2021) or (Nicastro & Carillo, 2021).",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A. Globally, about one-third of the food produced for human consumption is lost or wasted each year (FAO, 2021). This not only represents a significant loss of natural resources but also negatively impacts food security. Food loss typically occurs between production and distribution, especially in low-income countries, while food waste is more common at the consumer level in developed countries, particularly in retail and food service sectors (Parfitt, et al., 2011). Preventing food loss and waste is therefore a potential strategy to improve food security while reducing environmental impact and providing economic benefits to the different actors in the food supply chain.",
      "## Preventing Food Loss and Waste at Harvest and Production",
      "B. In the European Union, 19% of food waste comes from processing food (FUSIONS, 2019). To reduce this, using better processing methods, offering different package sizes, and using new packaging ideas can help. For example, Tesco in the UK is trying out new packaging that includes a strip that slows down fruit ripening, which could keep fruits fresh longer without costing shoppers more. Also, using smaller boxes for bananas has helped reduce waste and CO2 emissions because the bigger boxes were too large for some stores (WRAP, 2019).",
      "C. Improving transportation methods and reducing the distance food travels can also prevent loss. However, it's important to balance this with environmental considerations. As Wakeland (2012) points out more frequent, smaller deliveries might increase the carbon footprint. Ensuring clear communication within the food supply chain is also crucial to avoid imbalances between supply and demand, which can lead to food loss (Beretta, 2013)",
      "## Preventing Food Loss and Waste at Retail",
      "D. In the European Union, about 5 million tonnes of food waste happen in the retail sector each year (FUSIONS, 2019). In developing countries, poor market conditions like not enough space and bad hygiene lead to a lot of food waste. Simple fixes like adding roofs to protect food can make a big difference (FAO, 2021). In developed countries, analysis carried out by Gunders (2012) showed that selling items close to their expiry date at lower prices has helped keep customers happy without reducing sales. Retailers like Sainsbury’s and Tesco have introduced \"Buy one, get one later\" campaigns, which allows customers to receive a second identical product later for free. This helps to reduce the impulse of over-purchasing from typical “Buy one, get one free” campaigns.",
      "## Preventing Food Loss and Waste at Consumption",
      "E. In Europe, households are responsible for over 50% of all food waste, which is about 47 million tonnes each year (FUSIONS, 2019). This often happens because people don't realize how many resources are needed to make and sell food, and because stores encourage buying more than needed (Flanagan et al., 2019). Campaigns like “Love Food, Hate Waste” by WRAP and “Save Food Cut Waste” in Singapore play a crucial role in educating consumers about reducing waste through practical tips and raising awareness about the environmental and social impacts of food waste (UNEP 2021).",
      "## Redistribution of Food",
      "F. According to the food waste hierarchy, the best approach is to prevent food waste. This means we should try to avoid making too much food in the first place. When the production of excess food is unavoidable, it is important to focus on giving this food to people who need it. This not only helps fight hunger but also stops the food from being used in less useful ways, like for industrial purposes or being thrown away in landfills (EU Platform on Food Losses & Food Waste, 2019).",
      "G. Many organizations worldwide collect food from companies, food services, or retailers that would otherwise go to waste (BCFN 2021). They give this food to places that help people in need. For example, the “Buon Fine Coop” initiative has been working since 1990 to reduce food surpluses from Coop supermarkets by donating them to groups like the “Fondazione Banco Alimentare Onlus.” These groups then use the food to make meals or give it as ingredients to help those who are hungry. All the food given away is still good to eat but might be removed from shelves because it doesn't meet quality standards or is close to its expiry date (Caldeira et al., 2019). Therefore, when we can't prevent making too much food, it is crucial to prioritize giving it to people before using it for other purposes.",
      "## Conclusion",
      "H. Reducing food loss and waste is vital for improving food security, saving natural resources, and lessening environmental damage. From better processing and packaging at the production stage to smarter buying campaigns for consumers, many steps are being taken to tackle this global issue. Also, giving away extra food is not just about reducing waste; it's also about reducing hunger. By continuing to find new ways and apply these methods throughout the food supply chain, we can progress towards a world that is more sustainable and food-secure world."
    ],
