    "id": "berti",
    "short": "Reading · Berti et al.",
    "title": "The transformative potential of food banks",
    "cite": "Berti et al. (2021)",
    "kind": "Core reading · mediation",
    "reference": "Adapted from: Berti, G., Giordano, C., & Mininni, M. (2021). Assessing the transformative potential of food banks: The case study of Magazzini Sociali (Italy). Agriculture, 11(3), Article 249. https://doi.org/10.3390/agriculture11030249",
    "citeAs": "Cite as Berti et al. (2021) or (Berti et al., 2021).",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A. Food insecurity refers to when people and families do not consistently have enough healthy food (Dowler & O’Connor, 2012). This is a significant problem in wealthy countries, where more people are finding it hard to eat well every day (Long et al., 2020). At the same time, in the European Union (EU), about 88 million tonnes of food are thrown away each year, costing around 143 billion euros (Stenmarck et al., 2020). This situation, where there is both food insecurity and food waste, is known as the “food paradox” (Galli et al., 2019; Campiglio & Rovati, 2009). It demonstrates a worrying contradiction in wealthy societies and is considered highly unethical today.",
      "B. Redistributing excess food to those who need it is often presented as a solution to the food paradox (Caplan 2016). Central to these efforts are “food banks” which generally are community-organisations led by non-profit organizations based on volunteering. Food banks collect extra food from a variety of donors, including farmers, supermarkets, restaurants and private individuals (Santini & Cavicchi, 2014). The collected food is then stored and redistributed to people in need, avoiding any food waste or misuse. Food banks are therefore presented as a win–win solution to tackle the food paradox.",
      "C. In Italy, the community-based food bank Magazzini Sociali, has been redistributing donated food since 2015. This organisation operates in a region of the country with high rates of unemployment and poverty. It emerged as a way to support local families by collecting unused food and to distributing it to those in need. As of June 2020, 39 tons of food had been collected, corresponding to 5800 donations and 4300 deliveries to families. The most frequently donated products are other meals, bread, milk, and juices. Cooked food is collected and donated as well if it has not been handled and served. The volunteers portion the food into single trays, store it in insulated trays, and transport it. If the food isn't donated the same day, they freeze it immediately. The food collection area is limited to within 30 minutes of the food hub to ensure freshness.",
      "D. Although Magazzini Sociali has undoubtedly been successful in supporting the local community, food banks remain controversial. McMichael (2009) points out that food waste and food insecurity are the result of structural problems in the global food system. Large agricultural companies that prioritise profit over human wellbeing create unnecessary waste while making access to healthy, affordable food difficult for disadvantaged groups. While food banks can provide some immediate help to people in need, they cannot solve the underlying inequalities in the food production system that cause and sustain food insecurity. At best, food banks can only offer a band-aid solution to a much larger problem.",
      "E. Despite such criticism, food banks can still play an important role in improving the food system by working in partnership with governments. Food banks, such as Magazzini Sociali, have demonstrated that they are able to develop efficient and transparent food distribution programs. They therefore have expertise that could help governments to develop programs and policies that reduce food waste and improve access to nutritious food for those in need. In addition, financial support from the government could also help food banks to ramp up their operations so that they are able to support a larger number of people than is possible through charity alone. At present, the growth of food banks is limited by their reliance on volunteers and donations. Greater collaboration with governments could therefore increase the impact of food banks on the food system.",
      "F. In conclusion, the presence of both food insecurity and food waste in wealthy areas like the European Union presents a troubling situation where too much food exists alongside widespread hunger. Food banks such as Magazzini Sociali have done well in redistributing extra food to those who need it, but they can't solve the deeper issues causing food insecurity by themselves. Their work does however show that such organizations can be key in larger efforts to improve food systems. Working together, food banks and government agencies can use their strengths to make a bigger difference."
    ],
